title: Resin Identification Code
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

The little number inside a triangle of arrows on the bottom of a plastic tub.

The plastics industry made it in 1988 so sorters could tell one plastic from another. It says what the thing is made of. It does not say the thing can be recycled.

But the number sits inside the recycling arrows, so that is how everybody reads it. The standards body that looks after the codes says plainly that they are not recycling codes.

California passed a law in 2021 (SB 343) saying the arrows can't go around the number unless the item really does get recycled in the state. A plain solid triangle is the alternative. Check where enforcement stands before repeating this; it was being challenged in court in 2026.

A true label, read wrongly by nearly everyone, because of what it is drawn inside.

See [[Rethinking the Recycling Symbol]], [[Recyclable, but really?]], [[Polyethylene]], [[PLA]], [[Greenwashing]]

